# Tmp_tables: 0 and both size figures 0.
#
[log_start.inc] percona_slow_query_log.tts_no_tmp_table
[log_stop.inc] percona_slow_query_log.tts_no_tmp_table
Case 1 passed
#
# Case 2 (ACCURACY): MEMORY engine, 200-row materialised derived table.
# The tmp table holds 200 rows of 105 bytes, so the reported size should
# be at least 200 * 105 and not wildly above it.  Nothing goes to disk,
# so Tmp_disk_table_sizes must be 0.
#
[log_start.inc] percona_slow_query_log.tts_memory_derived_200
[log_stop.inc] percona_slow_query_log.tts_memory_derived_200
Case 2 passed
#
# Case 3 (ACCURACY): same shape, 2000 rows instead of 200.  Ten times the
# data must be reflected in the reported size.
#
[log_start.inc] percona_slow_query_log.tts_memory_derived_2000
[log_stop.inc] percona_slow_query_log.tts_memory_derived_2000
Case 3 passed
#
# Case 4 (ACCURACY): same 2000 rows, but a narrow tmp table (one INT
# column instead of INT + CHAR(100)).  It must report fewer bytes than
# case 3.
#
[log_start.inc] percona_slow_query_log.tts_memory_derived_2000_narrow
[log_stop.inc] percona_slow_query_log.tts_memory_derived_2000_narrow
Case 4 passed
#
# Case 5 (ACCURACY): two materialised derived tables of 200 rows each in
# one statement.  The figure is a sum over all tmp tables, so it must be
# close to twice the case 2 figure rather than equal to it.
#
[log_start.inc] percona_slow_query_log.tts_memory_two_tmp_tables
[log_stop.inc] percona_slow_query_log.tts_memory_two_tmp_tables
Case 5 passed
#
# Case 6 (INVARIANT): TempTable engine, 2000-row materialised derived
# table.  A non-empty tmp table must report a non-zero size, and an
# in-memory one must report no disk bytes.
#
[log_start.inc] percona_slow_query_log.tts_temptable_derived_2000
[log_stop.inc] percona_slow_query_log.tts_temptable_derived_2000
Case 6 passed
#
# Case 7 (ACCURACY): two statements that build the *same* 20-row tmp
# table, one from a 20-row source and one by de-duplicating 2000 rows
# down to 20.  The resulting tmp table is identical, so the reported
# sizes must be close.  A large gap means the figure counts cumulative
# allocations rather than the size of the tmp table.
#
[log_start.inc] percona_slow_query_log.tts_temptable_distinct_20_of_20
[log_stop.inc] percona_slow_query_log.tts_temptable_distinct_20_of_20
[log_start.inc] percona_slow_query_log.tts_temptable_distinct_20_of_2000
[log_stop.inc] percona_slow_query_log.tts_temptable_distinct_20_of_2000
Case 7 passed
#
# Case 8 (ACCURACY): force the in-memory tmp table to overflow to InnoDB
# on disk.  2000 rows of 105 bytes end up on disk, so the reported disk
# size should be in that ballpark rather than near the 16K in-memory
# limit, and the total must exceed it by the memory the table held
# before it spilled.
#
Warnings:
Warning	48040	Tmp_table_size is set below 1MiB. The TEMPTABLE engine requires at least 1MiB table size, and will use that value instead. Please increase the limit to silence this warning.
[log_start.inc] percona_slow_query_log.tts_disk_spill_2000
[log_stop.inc] percona_slow_query_log.tts_disk_spill_2000
Case 8 passed
#
# Case 9 (INVARIANT): an on-disk tmp table occupies whole InnoDB pages,
# so the disk figure must be a multiple of innodb_page_size.
#
